Date: Mon, 27 Sep 1993 01:16:52 -0400
From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: HEX: Backing the Thorne

  Some notes on dollar reputation to 'back' the Thorne.
 
1. Brian suggested that we don't have to register $'s reputation,
   and people just can buy / sell Thornes to each other.
 
   - Agreed, but we'd still need a mechanism for determining the current
Exchange value in more or less real time to make it look like a reasonable
market.  Registering the $'s reputation is the easiest way  I see.
 
2.  Anton writes:
 
>  While that would give the Thorne some backing,
>  it seems to me that the dollar reputation, as the only one with backing,
>  would rapidly usurp the other functions of the Exchange.
 
   'Backing the Thorne' will just make it a convertible currency.
The fact that Canadian $'s are exchangeable into $US, doesn't _give_
them value/backing, neither it makes all Canadian finansists to abandon
the stock market and trade nothing but dollars.  In fact, if they did that,
the Canadian dollar would lose it's value right after the Canadian stocks.
Same here.
 
   If Thornes are exchangeable into $s, so are all reputations; everything
is 'backed' equally.  (Backing is actually an incorrect word here).
Hex stops being an isolated market, and the Thorne turns from play money
into a freely tradeable alternative currency.
--------------------------------------------
 
   I am not saying that this should necessarily be done; these are just
a couple of guest ideas that visited my mind lately; they represent
themselves; I don't want to be associated with them.
 
   The reason why I am not registering $'s reputation myself:
lack of interest on anybody's part.  I figured I share the suggestion;
if people are interested, they'll do it, if not - I wont' waste my time.
 
